---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import PlantExample from '../../components/PlantExample.astro';

// Four actors look at the same Alder Creek unit, each with their own question
const actors = [
  {
    actor: 'Operator',
    question: 'Are my turbines on schedule?',
    data: 'Hour counters, last inspection, manufacturer interval',
    finding: 'Gas turbine 2 has run 8% past its inspection interval, while the plan schedules it for 2027.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Due diligence firm',
    question: 'Is the seller’s maintenance plan credible?',
    data: 'Planned schedule, completed inspections, actual durations and costs',
    finding: 'The 2027 inspection is planned at 12 days and 420 kUSD; the same one in 2025 took 15 days and cost 465 kUSD.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Bank',
    question: 'What does maintenance cost the output that repays the debt?',
    data: 'Unit capacity, 2×1 configuration, production plan',
    finding: '306 MW lost during the outage: 2.4% of annual output according to the plan, 3% according to history.',
  },
  {
    actor: 'Trader',
    question: 'Which output have I sold that I will not be able to deliver, and when?',
    data: 'Outage calendar, lost capacity, forward sales, price curve over the period',
    finding: 'About 22,000 MWh to buy back if the inspection takes 15 days instead of 12.',
  },
];
---
<Page
  title="Ontology for Energy Assets"
  description="An ontology for energy assets: assets, findings, engagements and lenders, connected and reviewable."
  eyebrow="Solutions · Ontology for Energy Assets"
  heading="Assets, findings and lenders, connected."
  lead="ArborSpace ships with a first domain model for energy assets and due diligence engagements, so the firm starts from its own vocabulary, not from a blank page."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap doc-layout">
      <nav class="doc-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <p class="eyebrow">Contents</p>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#use-case">Use case</a></li>
          <li><a href="#ontology">The ontology</a></li>
          <li><a href="#actors">The actors</a></li>
          <li><a href="#questions">The questions</a></li>
          <li><a href="#example">Example</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
      <article class="prose">
        <h2 id="use-case">Use case</h2>
        <p>
          A similarity engine matches each new case with the firm’s previous ones, and every
          conclusion traces back to a named rule that a lender’s reviewer can read.
        </p>

        <h2 id="ontology">The ontology</h2>
        <p>The ontology has three layers.</p>

        <h3><span class="layer-num">1</span> The vocabulary: what exists</h3>
        <p>The classes of objects in the firm’s business:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Assets:</strong> site, gas turbine, steam turbine, heat recovery steam generator, generator, equipment model, manufacturer.</li>
          <li><strong>Engagements:</strong> due diligence engagement, independent engineer’s report (IER), portfolio.</li>
          <li><strong>Parties:</strong> lender, sponsor, operator.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3><span class="layer-num">2</span> The links: how it holds together</h3>
        <p>Typed relations, which are never confused with one another:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>“GT1 feeds HRSG1” is a <strong>physical</strong> link.</li>
          <li>“GT1 is a 7F model” is a <strong>manufacturer</strong> link.</li>
          <li>“The engagement covers the site” and “the lender finances the project” are <strong>contractual and financial</strong> links.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These links are what lead from an inspection to its effect on the debt.</p>

        <h3><span class="layer-num">3</span> The rules: what a senior engineer checks</h3>
        <p>
          For example, “every planned outage duration is compared with similar outages already
          completed”, or “every maintenance deferral requires a signed approval”. Each rule
          produces findings that an engineer validates.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <p class="eyebrow">The example that makes it concrete</p>
          <p>
            A line in the maintenance plan (“GT1 inspection, 12 days”) says nothing on its own. The
            ontology knows that GT1 feeds a boiler that feeds the shared steam turbine. It deduces
            that the unit loses 306 MW, not a third of its output, and knows which lender carries
            that risk. No document in the data room contains this path; the ontology does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>What it is not</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Not a document store:</strong> the reports stay where they are.</li>
          <li><strong>Not a fixed IT data model:</strong> the classes are data, which the firm evolves itself.</li>
          <li><strong>Not an AI that guesses:</strong> everything is declared, versioned and verifiable.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="actors">The actors</h2>
        <p>
          The operator, the due diligence firm, the bank and the trader look at the same unit, but
          each needs different data.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Actor</th><th scope="col">The data they need</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {actors.map((a) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{a.actor}</th><td>{a.data}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="questions">The questions</h2>
        <p>
          Each actor asks their own question. Brought together in one model, the data gives four
          answers in a few clicks.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col">Actor</th><th scope="col">Their question</th><th scope="col">What they find at Alder Creek</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {actors.map((a) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{a.actor}</th><td>{a.question}</td><td>{a.finding}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <PlantExample />
</Page>
